{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>My lords, if now I have found favor in your eyes</b> — We find it written with a qamatz, and he called them by the name of their Master with אָדוֹנָי (Adonai), for he recognized in them that they are angels of the Most High, just as they are called Elohim and Elim, and therefore he prostrated himself to the ground." "<b>Do not pass by, please, from before your servant.</b> With each individual he would speak in the manner of the entire Torah — \"And you shall keep all My statutes (and do them); the nakedness of your father and the nakedness of your mother you shall not uncover\" (Leviticus 18:5, 18:7), \"And when you reap the harvest of your land, you shall not completely reap\" (Leviticus 19:9), \"And from there you will seek HaShem your Elohim, and you will find, when you search for Him with all your heart and with all your soul\" (Deuteronomy 4:29) — and most of the repetition of the Torah is thus, and the opposite of this is \"See, I place before you today\" (Deuteronomy 11:26). And our Sages said (Bereshit Rabbah 48:6): he said this to the greatest among them. And this too is possible, that he said to the greatest one, \"Do not pass by, please,\" and you and your companions who remain with you shall wash your feet. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he called all of them \"my lords\" and addressed each and every one individually: to the first he said, \"If now I have found favor in your eyes, do not pass by, please\"; and so he said to the second, and so he said to the third. To each one individually he would plead: \"If now I have found favor in your eyes, do not pass by from before your servant, and let now a little water be taken, and wash your feet, all of you.\" And this is the way of courtesy and honor, out of his great desire to host them. Now, he recognized in them that they are travelers and that they have no desire to lodge there, and therefore he asked of them only that a little water be taken to wash their feet a little because of the heat, to pour cold water upon a weary soul, and that they lean under the tree in the day's breeze — they would not enter a tent or dwelling."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban explains the qamatz vowel in אֲדֹנָי as indicating the plural form (\"my lords\"), while the text also carries the resonance of the divine name Adonai, since angels may be called by names of God.", 1 "The phrase 'most of the repetition of the Torah' (רוב משנה התורה) refers to legal passages where Moses addresses Israel in the second person, supporting the point that Scripture speaks to each individual as though directly addressed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:30:53.705015524Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}